        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The Urine Monitoring System (UMS) provides for collection, volume measurement, and sampling of individual urine collections in O-g or 1-g environments. UMS performance is significantly different in-flight than on the ground. Dilution is increased and inconsistent, and volume accuracy data are more erratic in-flight. Design modifications have been incorporated to improve performance and resolve existing in-flight anomalies. These modifications must be verified in-flight. In addition, information obtained from the in-flight video was used to evaluate the fluid dynamics of the phase separator.
<p>
The purpose of this experiment was to (1) evaluate the UMS design modifications by using in-flight calibration and sample collection to quantify accuracy, dilution, and cross-contamination parameters and (2) videotape in-flight function for analysis of fluid dynamics, including phase separation and dead volume.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
<i>Preflight</i>
<br>
The UMS operator received a familiarization training session, two general operation sessions, two calibration sessions, one void operation session, and two malfunction training sessions. The crewmember designated to videotape the calibration sessions attended the familiarization session and the two operation/calibration sessions at the same time as the UMS operator.
<p>
<i>In-Flight</i>
<br>
The UMS operator destowed and installed the UMS, which interfaces with the Waste Collection System (WCS) and attaches to the side hatch. The UMS was used five consecutive flight days (FDs) for the following studies:<br>
1. Calibration Sampling Fluid Dynamics - A total of 30 calibrations were performed.  After unstowing the calibration hardware items, the UMS operator began performing a series of calibration "runs." A run consisted of starting the UMS, dispensing the calibration fluid, and collecting two samples. A total of 30 runs were required, with no less than 6 and no more than 10 performed in a session. The camera operator was needed for 20 of the 30 runs to videotape various angles of the UMS during operation. <br>
2. UMS Operations - Performed on each of the 5 Test Days<br>
The UMS operator used the UMS for each void and recorded observations on a log. On one of the days that the anti-foam agent was not used, the UMS operator videotaped UMS operations during each void by activating the camcorder that was mounted nearby.
<br>
3. Anti-foam Test - Performed on 2 of the 5 Test Days
<br>
Before voiding, the UMS operator dispensed the contents of a premeasured syringe of anti-foaming agent into the UMS. On one of the two days, the antifoam agent was used, the UMS operator videotaped UMS operations for each void by activating the camcorder that was mounted nearby.
<br>
A log was provided for the UMS operator and/or the camera operator to record sample syringe numbers, Mission Elapsed Time (MET), and any comments or observations.
<br>
<i>Postflight</i>
<br>
Crewmembers were debriefed.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This was a hardware evaluation study. Results aren’t available.
<p>
